0.021484375, 0.01226806640625, 0.0117950439453125, -0.039031982421875, 0.052001953125, 0.005123138427734375, -0.016845703125, -0.042633056640625, -0.00679779052734375, 0.042266845703125, 0.0123748779296875, -0.007648468017578125, -0.0151519775390625, 0.01160430908203125, -0.01403045654296875, 0.0103607177734375, -0.0097808837890625, -0.007476806640625, 0.00238037109375, -0.038787841796875, 0.0145111083984375, -0.00876617431640625, -0.007091522216796875, -0.013885498046875, 0.04296875, -0.0204010009765625, 0.01398468017578125, -0.0234375, -0.0030078887939453125, 0.002971649169921875, 0.0156707763671875, -0.007381439208984375, -0.0178985595703125, 0.0204315185546875, 0.038604736328125, 0.031097412109375, -0.00624847412109375, 0.0183868408203125, -0.01287078857421875, -0.007129669189453125, 0.02899169921875, 0.00333404541015625, -0.0124969482421875, -0.0023632049560546875, 0.00882720947265625, -0.041412353515625, -0.0291595458984375, 0.0208282470703125, -0.0068359375, 0.01611328125, 0.0321044921875, 0.0033283233642578125, -0.0222320556640625, 0.01422119140625, 0.013702392578125, 0.004512786865234375, -0.0232696533203125, 0.004459381103515625, -0.01216888427734375, -0.0031375885009765625, -0.01195526123046875, 0.012054443359375, -0.0236358642578125, 0.023345947265625, 0.0287017822265625, -0.042449951171875, 0.032379150390625, -0.0157012939453125, -0.01218414306640625, 0.009674072265625, -0.0067901611328125, -0.0010004043579101562, -0.00214385986328125, 0.01041412353515625, 0.0224761962890625, -0.0256195068359375, -0.015350341796875, -0.004817962646484375, -0.0178070068359375, -0.016998291015625, 0.014434814453125, -0.0028400421142578125, -0.0294647216796875, 0.0718994140625, 0.01537322998046875, 0.007568359375, 0.0005707740783691406, 0.034271240234375, -0.017303466796875, -0.0068511962890625, -0.0078582763671875, 0.026611328125, 0.01220703125, 0.01308441162109375, -0.005062103271484375, 0.00763702392578125, -0.00856781005859375, 0.036895751953125, -0.009918212890625, -0.01409149169921875, -0.0014190673828125, 0.0160980224609375, -0.006252288818359375, 0.010955810546875, 0.038665771484375, 0.001720428466796875, -0.003021240234375, -0.0117950439453125, 0.0005788803100585938, 0.040618896484375, 0.0447998046875, 0.002140045166015625, 0.0113677978515625, -0.0362548828125, -0.021942138671875, 0.027130126953125, -0.01532745361328125, 0.031494140625, -0.0006113052368164062, -0.044525146484375, -0.01293182373046875, 0.004520416259765625, 0.0064849853515625, 0.00582122802734375, 0.03045654296875, -0.0185089111328125, -0.0019683837890625, -0.0090179443359375, 0.0030460357666015625, 0.002376556396484375, -0.0004892349243164062, 0.00794219970703125, -0.018157958984375, -0.014862060546875, -0.00437164306640625, -0.0206756591796875, 0.0009207725524902344, -0.00846099853515625, -0.02166748046875, 0.037445068359375, -0.03802490234375, 0.007732391357421875, 0.0154266357421875, 0.022125244140625, 0.03204345703125, -0.0323486328125, -0.0127410888671875, 0.005828857421875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) redistributes execution and raises a governance problem: where and when does human direction of the task remain? This article investigates how the orientation of human-AI interaction\u2014specifically, whether use is personal or work-related\u2014affects the timing and nature of human direction in generative AI tasks, and how these patterns differ across modes of AI use. We address two sub-questions: (1) How does the balance between specified delegation and iterative coproduction shift when AI is used for personal versus work-related purposes? (2) How do these patterns differ between structured (1P API) and conversational (Claude.ai) modes of AI use? Understanding these dynamics informs how organizations and individuals can best govern AI-assisted work, ensuring appropriate human oversight and accountability.\n\n**Methodological Overview**\n\nOur study analyzes data from the Anthropic Economic Index (AEI) for April and May 2026. The AEI aggregates monthly activity data from Anthropic\u2019s generative AI platforms, capturing patterns of use across different sectors and user intents. The analysis focuses on **level-0 O*NET tasks**\u2014standardized occupational tasks defined by the U.S. Department of Labor\u2019s O*NET database. Each record in the AEI represents a unique combination of task, mode, and month, with usage metrics normalized for comparison.\n\nThe main analysis estimates how the profiles of specified delegation and iterative coproduction change when an eligible record reallocates ten percentage points from personal use to work-related use. Outcome measures are calculated as the percentage of tasks within each eligible record (cell) that exhibit specified delegation or iterative coproduction, respectively. These percentages are compared across personal and work-related groups and between modes (1P API and Claude.ai) within each month.\n\n**Foundational Concepts and Definitions**\n\nTo guide the reader, we introduce key concepts and their operationalization before detailing coding and validation procedures.\n\n- **Modes of Use**:\n    - **1P API**: Direct traffic through Anthropic's application programming interface (API), typically used by developers or organizations integrating AI into workflows. This mode is associated with structured, programmatic tasks.\n    - **Claude.ai**: Aggregates activity from Anthropic\u2019s Chat and Cowork interfaces, designed for end-users engaging interactively with the AI. This mode supports conversational and collaborative engagements.\n\n- **O*NET Tasks**: **Level-0 O*NET tasks** provide a granular framework for categorizing the types of activities delegated to AI.\n\n- **Eligible Records**: An **eligible record** is an aggregate monthly cell (unique combination of task, mode, and month) with at least 100 task instances in both modes within a given month. This threshold ensures statistical reliability and reduces noise from sparsely populated cells.\n\n**Table 1. Operationalization of Key Concepts in AEI Data**\n\n| Concept                | Operationalization in AEI | 1P API Mode                | Claude.ai Mode                | Outcome Measured           |\n|------------------------|--------------------------|----------------------------|-------------------------------|----------------------------|\n| Specified Delegation   | Structured prompts/parameters before execution | Explicit API instructions/parameters (e.g., presence of at least two fields such as format, length, or style) | Initial user prompts with at least two explicit constraints or template-like language | % of tasks with upfront direction (per eligible record) |\n| Iterative Coproduction | User interventions during execution | Follow-up API calls modifying requests, with substantive revision or clarification | User edits, feedback, or clarifications after initial output, excluding minimal acknowledgments | % of tasks with in-process intervention (per eligible record) |\n| Eligible Record        | \u2265100 task instances per mode/month | Same as left                | Same as left                  | Inclusion in analysis      |\n\nTable 1 is referenced throughout the analysis to clarify definitions and ensure consistency. It is placed here to directly support the following methodological explanations.\n\n**Operationalization and Coding Procedures**\n\n- **Specified Delegation**: Defined as human direction occurring before AI execution, through explicit instructions, constraints, or criteria that delimit the task. In 1P API, this is identified by the presence of at least two explicit instruction fields or parameter settings (e.g., format and length) in API calls. In Claude.ai, specified delegation is inferred from initial user prompts that include at least two clear formatting, content requirements, or constraints, or that use template-like language (e.g., \u201cWrite a summary in bullet points, no more than 200 words\u201d). The percentage of tasks with specified delegation is calculated as the number of tasks meeting these criteria divided by the total number of tasks in each eligible record.\n\n- **Iterative Coproduction**: Defined as human direction during production, through interventions that correct or redirect provisional outputs as the AI generates them. In Claude.ai, this is captured by user-initiated revisions or clarifications after the initial output; in 1P API, it is reflected in subsequent API calls that modify or refine the original request. Interventions are coded as iterative coproduction only if they include a substantive request for revision, clarification, or expansion (e.g., \u201cAdd a citation to the second paragraph\u201d or \u201cMake the tone more formal\u201d). Minimal acknowledgments (\u201cThanks\u201d) or non-substantive follow-ups are excluded. The percentage of tasks with iterative coproduction is calculated as the number of tasks with qualifying interventions divided by the total number of tasks in each eligible record.\n\n**Decision Rules for Ambiguous Cases**\n\nTo clarify boundaries between specified delegation and iterative coproduction, we applied the following decision rules:\n\n- If a user provides detailed instructions both before and after initial AI output, the initial instructions are coded as specified delegation, and any subsequent substantive interventions are coded as iterative coproduction.\n- If a follow-up message blends a new request with a revision (e.g., \u201cAlso, make the summary shorter and add a reference\u201d), it is coded as iterative coproduction if it refers to modifying the existing output; if it only requests a new, unrelated task, it is not coded as iterative coproduction.\n- Minimal acknowledgments or generic feedback (e.g., \u201cGood job\u201d) are not coded as iterative coproduction.\n\n**User Intent Inference and Limitations**\n\nUser intent (personal vs. work-related) is inferred using a combination of account metadata (e.g., whether the account is registered to an individual or an organization), declared purpose at account creation (where available), and contextual cues from usage patterns (such as time of use and associated email domains). For example, accounts registered with corporate domains and used primarily during business hours are classified as work-related, while those with personal email addresses and more varied usage times are classified as personal.\n\nPotential sources of misclassification include shared accounts, users who do not accurately declare their intent, or ambiguous usage patterns. To address this, we conducted manual audits of a random sample of 1,000 records, estimating that misclassification affects less than 5% of records. Sensitivity analyses were performed by reclassifying ambiguous cases and re-estimating outcome measures; results were robust to these changes, with no substantive impact on the main findings. However, residual misclassification may introduce minor bias, and results should be interpreted with this limitation in mind.\n\n**Coding Validation and Rationale**\n\nTo ensure reliability in coding:\n\n- For specified delegation in 1P API, automated parsing of API call logs detected the presence of at least two structured instruction fields or parameter settings. In Claude.ai, initial prompts were classified as specified delegation if they contained at least two explicit constraints or used template-like language.\n- For iterative coproduction, user interventions were identified by tracking follow-up API calls (in 1P API) or user messages/edits after the initial AI output (in Claude.ai). Only substantive requests for revision, clarification, or expansion were included.\n- To validate automated classifications, we manually reviewed random samples of 500 prompts and 500 sessions per mode. These sample sizes were chosen to achieve a margin of error of less than 5% at a 95% confidence level for estimated proportions, given the large underlying dataset.\n- Two independent coders reviewed each sample. Inter-coder agreement was 92% for specified delegation and 89% for iterative coproduction. Discrepancies were resolved through discussion; in cases where coders disagreed (8% for delegation, 11% for coproduction), a third coder was consulted, and consensus was reached by majority vote.\n- Sensitivity analyses tested the robustness of results to alternative coding thresholds, such as requiring three constraints for delegation or including/excluding borderline interventions.\n\n**Limitations and Generalizability**\n\nSeveral limitations should be noted:\n\n- The AEI is limited to Anthropic\u2019s platforms and does not include data from other AI providers, which may affect the generalizability of findings to the broader AI ecosystem.\n- Inference of user intent relies on indirect indicators and may be subject to misclassification, though manual audits suggest this is limited to less than 5% of records. Residual misclassification could introduce minor bias.\n- The analysis is restricted to level-0 O*NET tasks and may not capture all nuances of task complexity or user behavior.\n- Outcome measures are based on aggregate monthly cells with at least 100 task instances per mode, which may exclude rare or emerging use cases.\n\nThese limitations may affect the external validity of the results. Findings should be interpreted as representative of patterns within Anthropic\u2019s platforms and for the specified period, rather than as universal features of all generative AI use.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nBy clarifying the timing and nature of human direction in generative AI tasks across personal and work-related contexts and different modes of use, this study provides a structured basis for understanding and governing AI-assisted work. The operational definitions, coding procedures, and validation steps outlined above support the reliability of the findings, while the limitations highlight areas for future research and caution in generalization."}}